message.content:
Below is a minimal “glue” example that shows how you would wire up the **tool** (`MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker`) inside a JUnit‑style unit test so that your *caller‑sensitive* helper actually gets exercised and verified.

> ⚠️ **Important:**  
> • The tool’s API accepts **strings**, not raw Java objects – this is because the underlying instrumentation framework serialises arguments into text form.  
> • It therefore makes sense to feed it either the fully–qualified name of the runtime object (e.g. its `.toString()` value when that already contains enough information) or simply the canonical class name if you only care about the *class* part.  

---

### 1️⃣ Test skeleton

```java
package com.example.myapp.test;

// ---- imports -------------------------------------------------------------
import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.*;          // optional – AssertJ
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationTargetException;
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// <-- YOUR CODE HERE -->
public class CallerSensitiveInjectionTest {

    @Test
    public void verifyInjectedInvokerIsCorrectlySet() throws Exception {
        // GIVEN : create / obtain the CSM instance under test
        CSM csmInstance = new CSM();                     // replace with real ctor / factory

        // WHEN  : invoke whatever triggers the injection logic
        //         (this step depends entirely on your application)
        csmInstance.doSomethingCallerSensitive();

        // THEN  : ask the tooling whether the injector was set properly
        //
        //   - first argument : a textual description of the CSM instance
        //     (the simplest way is to rely on .toString() – many frameworks expose useful info there).
        //   - second argument : the fully qualified name of the expected invoker class.
        //
        //   If you prefer to avoid calling .toString() directly, you may also serialize the whole
        //   object via Jackson/Gson etc.; however, most tests will find the default .toString()
        //   sufficient.
        //
        //   The returned value is typically a Boolean flag indicating success/failure.
        //   Adjust the assertion accordingly if the tool returns a different structure.
        //

        boolean injectSuccess =
                functions.MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker(
                        csmInstance.toString(),
                        MyExpectedClass.class.getCanonicalName());

        assertThat(injectSuccess)
                .as("Injector should match %s",
                    MyExpectedClass.class.getCanonicalName())
                .isTrue();
    }
}
```

#### What each line does

| Line | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `CM csmInstance = new CSM();` | Create the subject-under-test. Replace with your own construction logic. |
| `csmInstance.doSomethingCallerSensitive();` | Trigger the path where the caller‑sensitive method performs the injection. |
| `functions.MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker(...)` | Call the external validation routine supplied by the platform. |
| `assertThat(...).isTrue();` | Verify that the invocation succeeded. |

---

### 2️⃣ Adapting to your environment

If your project uses another testing library (JUnit 5 vs. JUnit 4, Hamcrest, Truth, …) feel free to swap out the assertions. The key point remains:

```java
boolean ok = functions.MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker(
                 <textual representation of csmInstance>,
                 MyExpectedClass.class.getCanonicalName());
```

Make sure that:
* `<textual representation>` really identifies the exact instance you expect.  
  For complex cases you might build a custom serializer that emits the relevant fields.  
* You import the correct package for `functions`. Depending on your IDE setup you may need to add a generated stub file or adjust the import statement.

---

### 3️⃣ Common pitfalls & quick fixes

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|--------|--------------|-----|
| Tool throws `NullPointerException` | Either `csmInstance` or `MyExpectedClass` is null at call time | Ensure both variables are initialised before invoking the tool. |
| Assertion fails even though manual inspection looks fine | The tool compares *stack order*, not merely presence | Double‑check that no other unrelated frames appear between the caller and the target frame. |
| Return type isn’t Boolean | The tool may wrap results in a Map/JSON payload | Inspect the documentation or log output; adapt parsing accordingly. |

---

### 4️⃣ Quick sanity‑test script

If you’d rather run a tiny standalone demo instead of embedding everything in a full Maven/Jenkins pipeline, try this simple main program:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    CSM csm = new CSM();
    csm.doSomethingCallerSensitive();

    System.out.println(functions.MethodInvokeTest_checkInjectedInvoker(
            csm.toString(),
            MyExpectedClass.class.getCanonicalName()));
}
```

Run it locally → you’ll see `"true"` printed if everything lines up.

---

Happy testing! 🎉

